From: Jason Germino <jasongermino@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 08 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: typec: tcpm: force DP mux at SRC_READY for passive
 USB-C→DP cable

A passive USB-C→DisplayPort cable connects a self-powered DP display
(which sources 5 V on Vbus) but has no USB PD controller.  Every
Source_Capabilities transmission returns TCPC_TX_FAILED (no GoodCRC)
so pd_capable stays false after nCapsCount retries.  Without a PD
session there are no VDMs, so the displayport.c altmode driver never
runs and the typec mux stays in TYPEC_STATE_USB forever.

Detect this case at SRC_READY:
  - pd_capable == false: no GoodCRC was ever received
  - ufp_self_powered == true: Vbus was already present when we entered
    SRC_ATTACHED, i.e. the UFP sources Vbus itself (self-powered display)

ufp_self_powered is recorded in SRC_ATTACHED right after
tcpm_src_attach(), using the same signal as the preceding patch.  It
cannot be derived from port->vbus_present at SRC_READY: by then our own
Vbus supply has made vbus_present true for every attached device, so
that test would also match ordinary non-PD USB peripherals and force
them into DP mode.

When detected, call tcpm_mux_set() with TYPEC_DP_STATE_E (4-lane DP,
no USB SuperSpeed) directly, bypassing the VDM negotiation path that
will never succeed.

The sunxi-phy-switcher driver receives this call (data == NULL) and
synthesises HPD=asserted — see the companion patch to that driver —
which causes the combophy to fire its serdes extcon so edp0 starts
DisplayPort link training.

Signed-off-by: Jason Germino <jasongermino@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpm.c
@@ -30,6 +30,7 @@
 #include <linux/usb/role.h>
 #include <linux/usb/tcpm.h>
 #include <linux/usb/typec_altmode.h>
+#include <linux/usb/typec_dp.h>
 
 #include <uapi/linux/sched/types.h>
 
@@ -337,6 +338,15 @@
 	bool vbus_present;
 
 	/*
+	 * Set on SRC_ATTACHED when Vbus was already present before our own
+	 * regulator could have raised it, i.e. the attached UFP is self-powered
+	 * and sources Vbus itself (e.g. a DP display).  Our own supply also makes
+	 * vbus_present true later on, so vbus_present alone cannot tell the two
+	 * cases apart once the port is in SRC_READY.
+	 */
+	bool ufp_self_powered;
+
+	/*
 	 * Set to true when vbus is less than VSAFE0V max.
 	 * Set to false when vbus is greater than VSAFE0V max.
 	 */
@@ -4035,6 +4045,7 @@
 
 	case SRC_ATTACHED:
 		ret = tcpm_src_attach(port);
+		port->ufp_self_powered = ret >= 0 && port->vbus_present;
 		if (ret < 0) {
 			tcpm_set_state(port, SRC_UNATTACHED, 0);
 		} else if (port->vbus_present) {
@@ -4198,6 +4209,31 @@
 		 * port type-c and the spec does not clearly say whether PD is
 		 * possible when type-c is connected to Type-A/B
 		 */
+
+		/*
+		 * Passive USB-C->DisplayPort cable: pd_capable stays false
+		 * because the display has no PD controller (every
+		 * Source_Capabilities TX returns TCPC_TX_FAILED, no GoodCRC).
+		 * The self-powered display already had Vbus up when we attached
+		 * (ufp_self_powered); this is what separates it from an ordinary
+		 * non-PD peripheral.  port->vbus_present cannot be used here: by
+		 * SRC_READY it is true for every attach because of our own supply.
+		 * Switch the mux to 4-lane DP (STATE_E) directly instead of
+		 * waiting for VDMs that will never come.
+		 */
+		tcpm_log(port, "OctaneOS 1008: SRC_READY pd_capable=%d vbus_present=%d self_powered=%d",
+			 port->pd_capable, port->vbus_present, port->ufp_self_powered);
+		if (!port->pd_capable && port->ufp_self_powered) {
+			enum typec_orientation orientation;
+
+			tcpm_log(port, "OctaneOS 1008: non-PD self-powered sink, forcing DP mode");
+			if (port->polarity == TYPEC_POLARITY_CC1)
+				orientation = TYPEC_ORIENTATION_NORMAL;
+			else
+				orientation = TYPEC_ORIENTATION_REVERSE;
+			tcpm_mux_set(port, TYPEC_DP_STATE_E,
+				     USB_ROLE_HOST, orientation);
+		}
 		break;
 	case SRC_WAIT_NEW_CAPABILITIES:
 		/* Nothing to do... */
